🧠 Deep Learning Experiments: CNN and RNN

📌 Overview

This repository contains hands-on deep learning experiments implemented using Python, TensorFlow, Keras, and NumPy in Google Colab.

It explores two important neural network architectures: Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) for image classification and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) for sequential data processing.

These experiments demonstrate fundamental deep learning concepts, model architecture, training, evaluation, prediction, and performance visualization.

🧪 Experiments

1. Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) for Image Classification

Objective: Build a CNN model to classify images into different categories using the CIFAR-10 dataset.

Dataset: CIFAR-10

The dataset contains 60,000 RGB images of size 32 × 32 pixels across 10 classes:

- ✈️ Airplanes
- 🚗 Cars
- 🐦 Birds
- 🐱 Cats
- 🦌 Deer
- 🐕 Dogs
- 🐸 Frogs
- 🐎 Horses
- 🚢 Ships
- 🚚 Trucks

Key Concepts:

- Image preprocessing and pixel normalization
- Convolutional layers ("Conv2D")
- Max pooling ("MaxPooling2D")
- Feature extraction and flattening
- Fully connected layers ("Dense")
- Softmax-based classification
- Model training, evaluation, and accuracy visualization

Workflow:

1. Load the CIFAR-10 dataset.
2. Normalize image pixel values.
3. Build and compile the CNN architecture.
4. Train the model for 10 epochs.
5. Evaluate test performance.
6. Visualize training and validation accuracy.
7. Predict image categories.

Technologies: Python, TensorFlow, Keras, Matplotlib, Google Colab.

2. Recurrent Neural Network (RNN): Basic to Advanced

Objective: Understand recurrent neural networks and explore their applications in sequential data processing, time-series forecasting, and text classification.

Key Concepts:

- Sequential data and hidden states
- Vanilla RNN forward propagation using NumPy
- Time-series data preparation using sliding windows
- SimpleRNN model training
- Model evaluation using MAE, MSE, and RMSE
- Training and validation loss visualization
- Comparison of SimpleRNN, LSTM, and GRU
- Bidirectional LSTM architecture
- Text sentiment classification using embeddings and LSTM
- Saving and loading trained models

Workflow:

1. Understand sequence data and recurrent computations.
2. Implement a vanilla RNN forward pass using NumPy.
3. Generate and prepare time-series data.
4. Train a SimpleRNN forecasting model.
5. Evaluate predictions using regression metrics.
6. Compare SimpleRNN, LSTM, and GRU architectures.
7. Explore Bidirectional LSTM.
8. Build an LSTM-based sentiment classification model.
9. Save and reload the trained model.

Technologies: Python, NumPy, TensorFlow, Keras, Matplotlib, Google Colab.

🛠️ Tech Stack

Technology| Purpose
Python| Programming language
NumPy| Numerical computations and RNN implementation
TensorFlow / Keras| Deep learning model development
Matplotlib| Data visualization
Google Colab| Cloud-based experimentation
CIFAR-10| CNN image classification dataset
Synthetic time-series data| RNN forecasting experiments
Text data| LSTM sentiment classification

🚀 How to Run

1. Open "Google Colab" (https://colab.research.google.com/).
2. Open the relevant experiment notebook from this repository.
3. Select a suitable runtime. A GPU may accelerate CNN training when available.
4. Execute the notebook cells sequentially.
5. Observe the model outputs, evaluation metrics, and visualizations.
6. Record experimental observations and conclusions.

📊 Evaluation

CNN experiment

- Training accuracy
- Validation accuracy, if configured
- Test accuracy
- Training and validation loss

RNN experiment

- Mean Squared Error (MSE)
- Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
- Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
- Actual versus predicted time-series values
- Classification accuracy for sentiment analysis

Actual results depend on model architecture, hyperparameters, and training conditions. Refer to the notebook outputs for measured results.

🎯 Learning Outcomes

- Understand the fundamentals of CNNs and RNNs.
- Learn image and sequential data preprocessing.
- Explore convolution, pooling, hidden states, and recurrent computation.
- Build neural network models using TensorFlow and Keras.
- Implement a basic RNN forward pass using NumPy.
- Train, evaluate, and compare deep learning models.
- Visualize experimental results and interpret evaluation metrics.
- Explore applications in computer vision, time-series forecasting, and natural language processing.

🔮 Future Enhancements

- Improve CNN performance through data augmentation and hyperparameter tuning.
- Experiment with deeper CNN architectures.
- Implement Backpropagation Through Time (BPTT) from scratch.
- Investigate vanishing and exploding gradients in RNNs.
- Compare recurrent architectures on larger datasets.
- Explore attention mechanisms and sequence-to-sequence models.
- Develop additional experiments using real-world datasets.

